VT_TALK("sql/sql-where", {
 "where": "WHERE is the filter of SQL. The database takes each row, tests it with your condition, keeps it when the answer is true, and drops it otherwise. You can compare with equals, not equals, smaller and bigger. BETWEEN checks a range and includes both ends, so between one hundred and three hundred includes three hundred. IN checks against a short list of values, which is shorter than writing many equals signs joined by OR. Text must match exactly, including capital letters. Dates written as year, month, day compare correctly as text, which is why the sample shop stores them that way. Think of a security guard at a gate checking every person against one rule.",
 "logic": "Real questions often need more than one test. AND means both tests must be true, OR means at least one must be true, and NOT flips true into false. Here is the trap. SQL does AND before OR, the same way maths does multiplication before addition. So category equals sports OR category equals kitchen AND price below three hundred really means sports items of any price, plus cheap kitchen items. That is why the football at eight hundred and fifty sneaks into the first answer. Brackets fix it. Whenever AND and OR meet in one condition, add brackets so every reader sees the same meaning. Think of brackets in maths, two plus three times four is fourteen, not twenty.",
 "patterns": "LIKE finds values that match a pattern. The percent sign stands for any number of characters, even zero, and the underscore stands for exactly one character. So P percent finds every name starting with P, and percent box finds every name ending with box. In SQLite, LIKE ignores upper and lower case for plain English letters, while GLOB is case sensitive and uses star and question mark instead. PostgreSQL is the other way round, its LIKE is case sensitive and ILIKE ignores case. Missing values need IS NULL. Writing equals NULL finds nothing at all, because comparing with an unknown gives unknown. Think of LIKE as a crossword clue with blank squares.",
 "order": "ORDER BY sorts your result. ASC, which is the default, puts small values first, and DESC puts big values first. You can list several columns, and each later column only breaks ties in the earlier ones, just like a cricket points table that sorts by points and then by net run rate. In SQLite, NULL counts as smaller than everything, so missing values come first in ascending order. Since SQLite version three point thirty you can write NULLS LAST to move them to the end. Text sorts by character codes, so capital letters come before small letters unless you ask for COLLATE NOCASE. Think of lining up children by height and then by name when two are the same height.",
 "levels": "Let us climb the ladder once more. At the bottom, WHERE is a sieve and ORDER BY lines rows up. At college level, WHERE runs before the select list and ORDER BY runs after it, which is why ORDER BY can use an alias but WHERE cannot. At masters level, WHERE is selection in relational algebra, and a plain comparison on a column lets the planner use an index, while wrapping the column in a function often stops that. At research level, optimizers guess how many rows each test keeps, using statistics, and a bad guess makes a query slow. Think of a cook who checks the cheapest test first, like looking at the colour of a mango before cutting it open."
});
